column62:
That's because of the (visually) 'wrong' use of * (wrong in the sense that it's unintuitive). The key is that * is part of the <i>type</i> of the declaration, not of the variable; an int* is not an int. So<p><pre><code>  int *j = &i;
</code></pre>
is more correctly expressed and easier to understand when written like<p><pre><code>   int* j = &i;
</code></pre>
The only reason to put the * in front of the variable name is when declaring several pointers in one line. So the solution is to only use it that context, or not doing it at all.
